===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|uncountable|also|figurative}} [[increase#Noun|Increase]] by [[natural#Adjective|natural]] [[growth]], especially the [[gradual#Adjective|gradual]] [[increase#Noun|increase]] of [[organic#Adjective|organic]] [[body#Noun|bodies]] by the [[internal#Adjective|internal]] [[addition]] of [[matter#Noun|matter]]; organic growth; also, the [[amount#Noun|amount]] of such growth. {{defdate|from early 17th c.}}
#: {{synonyms|en|accrescence<q:archaic>}}
#: {{antonyms|en|nonaccretion}}
#* {{RQ:Chesnutt House Behind the Cedars|chapter=I|page=3|passage=Warwick was unable to perceive much change in the market-house. {{...}} There might have been a slight '''accretion''' of the moss and lichen on the shingled roof.}}
#* {{RQ:Wharton Age of Innocence|chapter=IV|page=25|passage=The immense '''accretion''' of flesh which had descended on her in middle life like a flood of lava on a doomed city had changed her from a plump active little woman with a neatly-turned foot and ankle into something as vast and august as a natural phenomenon.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} (Gradual) increase by an [[external#Adjective|external]] addition of matter; {{lb|en|countable}} an [[instance#Noun|instance]] of this.
#: {{synonyms|en|accrescence<q:archaic>}}
#: {{antonyms|en|nonaccretion}}
#: {{nearsyn|en|accumulation}}
#: {{ux|en|A mineral augments not by growth, but by '''accretion'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Bacon Sylva Sylvarum|chapter=Experiments in Consort, Touching the Affinities, and Differences, betweene Plants and Inanimate Bodies|page=154|para=602|passage={{...}} ''Plants'' doe nouriſh; ''Inanimate Bodies'' doe not: They haue an '''''Accretion''''', but no ''Alimentation''.}}
#* {{RQ:Hobbes Decameron|chapter=Of Gravity and Gravitation|page=94|passage=God hath from the beginning made all the Kindes of Hard, and Heavie, and Diaphanous Bodies that are, and of ſuch Figure and magnitude as he thought fit; but hovv ſmall ſoever, they may by '''accretion''' become greater in the Mine, or perhaps by generation, though vve knovv not hovv.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=“Ludwig” [pseudonym; {{w|Rufus Wilmot Griswold}}]|chapter=Edgar Poe|mainauthor=[[w:Nathaniel Parker Willis|N[athaniel] Parker Willis]]|title=Hurry-graphs; or, Sketches of Scenery, Celebrities and Society, Taken from Life|edition=2nd|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Charles Scribner's Sons|Charles Scribner]]|date=9 October 1849|year_published=1851|page=241|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/hurrygraphs00will/page/241/mode/1up|oclc=1024127881|passage=Suddenly starting from a proposition, exactly and sharply defined, in terms of utmost simplicity and clearness, he {{quote-gloss|{{w|Edgar Allan Poe}}}} rejected the forms of customary logic, and by a crystalline process of '''accretion''', built up his ocular demonstrations in forms of gloomiest and ghastliest grandeur, or in those of the most airy and delicious beauty—so minutely and distinctly, yet so rapidly, that the attention which was yielded to him was chained till it stood among his wonderful creations—till he himself dissolved the spell, and brought his hearers back to common and base existence, by vulgar fancies or exhibitions of the ignoblest passion.|footer={{small|Originally published in the ''{{w|New-York Tribune}}'', and now regarded as containing unfair characterizations of Poe.}}}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=w:Amelia Gere Mason|title=The Women of the French Salons: The Salons of the Eighteenth Century|journal=[[w:The Century Magazine|The Century Illustrated Monthly Magazine]]|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:The Century Company|The Century Co.]]; London: [[w:T. Fisher Unwin|T[homas] Fisher Unwin]]|month=August|year=1890|volume=XVIII (New Series; volume XL overall)|issue=4|page=596|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/century-1890-v-18/page/596/mode/1up|column=1|oclc=692496497|passage=Our {{quote-gloss|women's}} social life is largely a form, a whirl, a commercial relation, a display, a duty, the result of external '''accretion''', not of internal growth. It is not in any sense a unity, nor an expression of the best intellectual life, which seeks other channels.}}
#* {{RQ:London Burning Daylight|chapter=XI|page=106|passage=Two-story log buildings, in the business part of town, brought him from forty to fifty thousand dollars apiece. These fresh '''accretions''' of capital were immediately invested in other ventures.}}
#* {{RQ:Atlantic|author=Edward Tenner|title=Why Wikipedia's Fans Shouldn't Gloat|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20230603180822/https://www.theatlantic.com/technology/archive/2012/03/why-wikipedias-fans-shouldnt-gloat/254584/|date=16 March 2012|passage=Written by '''accretion''' rather than from a single author's interpretation Wikipedia has a neo-positivist mania for facts that devalues interpretation in depth, yet in matching [[w:Otto Friedrich|{{quote-gloss|Otto}} Friedrich]]'s review against [[w:Vladimir Nabokov|{{quote-gloss|Vladimir}} Nabokov]] it also shows that it is far from neutral.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Shoshana Zuboff|chapter=‘We Make Them Dance’: Surveillance Capitalism, the Rise of Instrumentarian Power, and the Threat to Human Rights|editor=Rikke Frank Jørgensen|title=Human Rights in the Age of Platforms|location=Cambridge, Mass.&#59; London|publisher=w:MIT Press|year=2019|page=25|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-24492/page/25/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-262-03905-5|passage=The systematic '''accretion''' of violence and complicity {{quote-gloss|during World War II}} that engulfed whole populations at extreme velocity invoked a kind of bewilderment that ended in paralysis, even for many of the greatest minds of the twentieth century.}}
## {{lb|en|geology}} The [[process#Noun|process]] by which [[material#Noun|material]] is [[add#Verb|added]] to a [[geological]] [[feature#Noun|feature]]; specifically, to a [[tectonic plate]] at a [[subduction zone]].
##: {{antonyms|en|attrition|erosion}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable|also|figurative}} ''Followed by'' '''[[of#Preposition|of]]''': external addition of matter to a [[thing]] which [[cause#Verb|causes]] it to grow, especially in amount or [[size#Noun|size]].
#* {{RQ:Steele Englishman|issue=2|date=8 October 1713|page=12|passage=[W]hile ſome fevv grevv rich by turning Money in their ovvn Banks, there vvas a falſe Appearance of VVealth vvithin, but no '''Accretion''' of Riches from abroad.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} The process of [[separate#Adjective|separate]] [[particle]]s [[aggregate#Verb|aggregating]] or [[coalesce|coalescing]] together; [[concretion]]; {{lb|en|countable}} a thing [[form#Verb|formed]] in this manner.
#: {{ux|en|the '''accretion''' of particles to form a solid mass}}
#* {{RQ:Hale Mankind|chapter=The Sixth Evidence of Fact Proving ''Novitatem Generis Humani'', Namely, the History of the ''Patres Familiarum'', and the Original Plantation of the Continents and Islands of the World|page=191|passage=[T]he vvhole Country of ''Holland'' ſeems to be an '''Accretion''' partly by the Sea, partly by the River ''Rhine''.}}
#* {{RQ:Hardy Tess|volume=I|chapter=XIII|page=166|passage=She had no fear of the shadows; her sole idea seemed to be to shun mankind—or rather that cold '''accretion''' called the world, which, so terrible in the mass, is so unformidable, even pitiable, in its units.}}
## {{lb|en|astrophysics}} The [[formation]] of [[planet]]s, [[star#Noun|stars]], and other [[celestial body|celestial bodies]] by the aggregating of matter [[draw#Verb|drawn]] together by [[gravity]]; also, the growth of a celestial body through this process.
##* {{RQ:Atlantic|author=Alexandra Witze|title=Earth may have been Formed by a Bunch of Tiny Space Pebbles|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20230128062024/https://www.theatlantic.com/science/archive/2018/04/planets-are-built-from-pebbles/558854/|date=26 April 2018|passage=This theory, known as pebble '''accretion''', is reshaping how scientists think about the early solar system. {{...}} "In many ways, pebble '''accretion''' is the most efficient way of adding mass to a body," says {{quote-gloss|Michiel}} Lambrechts.}}
# {{lb|en|countable|chiefly|figurative}} Something [[gradually]] added to or [[grow#Verb|growing]] on a thing [[externally]].
#: {{coi|en|an '''accretion''' of ice}}
#* {{RQ:Hale Mankind|chapter=A Brief Consideration of the Hypotheses that Concern the Eternity of the World|page=96|passage=[T]hoſe places, vvhich vvere formerly filled vvith VVood, have buried the fallen Trees three, four, or five foot deep in the ground, by an '''accretion''' or cover of Earth, derived to them ſometimes by Alluvions or Floods, {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:George Cornewall Lewis|chapter=History of Rome, from the Expulsion of the Kings to the Burning of the City by the Gauls (509–390 {{smallcaps|b.c.}})|title=An Inquiry into the Credibility of the Early Roman History{{nb...|[...] In Two Volumes.}}|location=London|publisher=[[w:John William Parker|John W[illiam] Parker and Son]],{{nb...|West Strand.}}|year=1855|volume=II|section=part I (From the Establishment of Consuls to the First Secession (509–494 {{smallcaps|b.c.}})), §&nbsp;17|page=75|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.49119/page/n89/mode/1up|oclc=969041677|passage=If therefore it is admitted that a large part of the narrative of [[w:Dionysius of Halicarnassus|Dionysius {{quote-gloss|of Halicarnassus}}]] is false, what good ground have we for believing the rest? Assuming however that we are to strip off all the subordinate parts of his narrative, as a later '''accretion''', and to retain only a nucleus of the leading facts, do we find that these can be safely accepted, and that he is confirmed in them by the agreement of the other historians? So far is this from being the case, that the accounts transmitted to us differ widely in the material points of the transaction.}}
## {{lb|en|[[conservation]] [[science#Noun|science]]}} A [[substance#Noun|substance]] which has [[build up#Verb|built up]] on the [[surface#Noun|surface]] of an [[object#Noun|object]], rather than [[become]] [[embed#Verb|embedded]] in it.
##: {{ux|en|'''Accretions''' of dirt on clothing are often left in place by conservators because they can provide additional details about the artefact’s importance or history.}}
# {{lb|en|law}}
## {{lb|en|uncountable}} Increase in [[property#Noun|property]] by the addition of other property to it (for example, [[gain#Noun|gain]] of [[land#Noun|land]] by {{l|en|alluvion|t=the [[deposition]] of [[sediment#Noun|sediment]] by a [[river#Noun|river]] or [[sea]]}} or {{l|en|dereliction|t=[[recession]] of [[water#Noun|water]] from the [[usual#Adjective|usual]] [[watermark#Noun|watermark]]}}, or [[entitlement]] to the [[product#Verb|products]] of the property such as [[interest#Noun|interest]] on [[money#Noun|money]]); or by the property [[owner]] [[acquire|acquiring]] another [[person#Noun|person]]'s [[ownership]] [[right#Noun|rights]]; [[accession#Noun|accession]]; {{lb|en|countable}} an instance of this.
##: {{synonyms|en|accrual<q:archaic>|accruer}}
## {{lb|en|uncountable}} Increase of an [[inheritance]] to an [[heir#Noun|heir]] or [[legatee]] due to the [[share#Noun|share]] of a [[coheir|co-heir]] or [[colegatee|co-legatee]] being added to it, because the latter person is [[legally]] [[unable#Adjective|unable]] to [[inherit]] the share.

